        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ios): restore in-flight runs after reconnect",
        "body": "Part of #100197. Supersedes the stacked `afterSeq` design in #100267.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen iOS backgrounds, reconnects, changes sessions, or misses an event sequence, it can lose ownership of a run that is still active on the Gateway. The transcript may look idle, Abort disappears, and a second send becomes possible while the first run is still working.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Gateway already exposes the canonical active-run snapshot through `chat.history.inFlightRun`; the TUI already consumes that contract. This rewrite removes every proposed `afterSeq` protocol/client change and restores ownership from the existing snapshot instead.\n\nThe shared Apple chat model now:\n\n- adopts active run ID and buffered text during bootstrap, foreground resume, sequence-gap recovery, immediate post-send refresh, delayed retries, and completion refreshes;\n- orders history snapshots against newer live deltas, terminal events, session messages, session switches, agent changes, and concurrent history requests;\n- keeps ownership stable when another client streams a different run in the same session;\n- scopes bare-global/default-agent aliases and non-default agent-qualified global sessions without cross-agent transcript adoption;\n- distinguishes current Gateway negative state via `sessionInfo.hasActiveRun` from legacy gateways that omit active-run metadata;\n- preserves live/provisional transcript rows when delayed history arrives, including identical replies from different turns;\n- rearms timeouts safely and clears silent completed/failed runs from authoritative current-Gateway state.\n\nCodex app-server source confirms a turn becomes `InProgress` with no message items before later `AgentMessageDelta` notifications (`codex-rs/app-server/src/bespoke_event_handling.rs:155`, `codex-rs/app-server-protocol/src/protocol/event_mapping.rs:359`). That is why an active run with an empty buffered string is still valid ownership.\n\n## User Impact\n\nReturning to iOS or macOS chat no longer loses an active response. Streaming text, tool state, Abort, and the send lock recover without adding a Gateway protocol or cursor, while older Gateways retain the prior transcript-based completion fallback.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `swift test --package-path apps/shared/OpenClawKit --filter ChatViewModelTests` — 128/128 passed after rebase.\n- `swift build --package-path apps/macos` — passed after rebase.\n- SwiftFormat lint with `config/swiftformat` — clean for every touched Swift file.\n- Final Codex autoreview — clean; no accepted/actionable findings.\n- Direct Gateway contract review: `src/gateway/server-methods/chat.ts`, `src/gateway/server-session-events.ts`, and `src/gateway/server-methods/session-active-runs.ts`.\n- Direct Codex contract review at `be33f80bc65159c094ecd06bf155afa3061ce23d`: `codex-rs/app-server/src/bespoke_event_handling.rs:155-183`, `codex-rs/app-server-protocol/src/protocol/event_mapping.rs:359-367`, and `codex-rs/app-server-protocol/src/protocol/v2/turn.rs:375-378`.\n\nVisual reconnect/background proof is requested separately on this exact head.\n",
